INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant, Kaspersky and WIRED reporting on 3CX 2023 compromise", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/3cx-software-supply-chain-compromise", "text": "Evidence batch: Mandiant reported that the initial compromise of 3CX's network came through malicious software downloaded from the Trading Technologies website: a tampered X_TRADER installer. The X_TRADER compromise preceded the 3CX follow-on compromise. Mandiant reconstructed lateral movement involving credential harvesting and use of Fast Reverse Proxy, followed by compromise of both Windows and macOS 3CX build environments. On Windows, TAXHAUL and COLDCAT persisted via IKEEXT DLL search-order hijacking with LocalSystem privileges; on macOS, POOLRAT persisted via Launch Daemons. The attacker then distributed trojanized 3CX DesktopApp versions, including 18.12.416 and earlier. The application contained SUDDENICON, which obtained C2 servers from encrypted GitHub icon files and downloaded ICONICSTEALER, a browser-information dataminer. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster, and assesses with moderate confidence that it relates to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity. Kaspersky reported the Gopuram backdoor as a later/final payload on selected victims, especially cryptocurrency companies, and attributed the campaign to Lazarus with medium-high confidence based on coexistence with AppleJeus, infrastructure overlap, and targeting. WIRED summarized Mandiant's chain as X_TRADER on a 3CX employee PC, lateral movement to a 3CX development server, corruption of a 3CX installer, and distribution to customers; it also reported naming overlap in public reporting (Kimsuky/Emerald Sleet/Velvet Chollima) but attribution remains assessment rather than adjudicated fact."}
